Found in a bug hunt, reproduced by the finder.

Git.pm:2461 -- `$self->_write_ref_oid( $_, $commit{$_} ) for @wanted;`

Phase one validates names and builds every commit, which is what #47 added.
But validate_board_ref only checks syntax and namespace; it cannot know
whether a name is writable against the refs already on the board. A
directory/file conflict (refs/karr/tasks/2 when refs/karr/tasks/2/data
exists) passes validation, passes commit construction, then dies in the
MIDDLE of the write loop -- after earlier refs were overwritten, before the
prune step.

Cmd::Restore's POD promises the opposite: "a snapshot karr cannot apply ...
is refused with the board exactly as it was."

Reproduction (hand-edited backup, which is the threat model
validate_board_ref was written for):

    before: #1 Alpha  #2 Beta  #3 Gamma
    karr restore --yes --input evil.yml
    karr: could not write refs/karr/tasks/2: cannot lock ref
          'refs/karr/tasks/2', there are refs beneath that folder   exit=1
    after:  #1 ALPHA-FROM-SNAPSHOT  #2 Beta  #3 Gamma

Task 1 took the snapshot's content, 2 and 3 kept the board's. The board is
a mixture that existed in neither place, and the error reads as "nothing
happened".

The D/F name needs a hand-edited backup, but the same half-apply follows
from ANY failure in that loop -- _write_ref_oid dies after 32 exhausted
attempts on a contended ref, which needs no hand editing at all, and
restore takes no lock while other agents keep writing.
